{"title":"Publishing a Quarto Project","markdown":{"headingText":"Publishing a Quarto Project","containsRefs":false,"markdown":"\nOnce you have created your Quarto project, you will need to publish the rendered output so that others can access it. Quarto supports several publishing options, but for most teaching-related content at the University of Bath, publishing to Moodle is the recommended approach.\n\n## Publish to Moodle\n\nFor teaching materials intended for University of Bath students, we recommend publishing your Quarto website or mini-site through Moodle.\n\nBenefits include:\n\n- Available directly to students through Moodle.\n- Included within the University's Moodle archive processes.\n- Covered by established institutional retention arrangements.\n- Access controlled through Moodle course permissions.\n- No requirement for students to visit external websites.\n\nQuarto websites generate a collection of HTML, CSS, JavaScript, image, and supporting files. These can be hosted within Moodle as a self-contained mini-site.\n\n\n### Moodle publishing workflow\n\n1. Render your Quarto project.\n2. Locate the generated `_site` or output folder.\n3. Create a ZIP archive containing the entire published site and all supporting files.\n4. In Moodle, create a **File** activity (not a Folder activity).\n5. Upload the ZIP file and use Moodle's **Unzip** option.\n6. Set the site's `index.html` file as the **Main File**.\n7. Save the activity and test that the site launches correctly.\n\nThe University of Bath Moodle currently has a 50 MB upload limit for file activities. If your published site exceeds this size please contact [tel@bath.ac.uk](mailto:tel@bath.ac.uk)\n\n::: {.callout-important}\n\nDetailed step-by-step guidance is available at [Publishing an HTML mini-site to Moodle](https://teachinghub.bath.ac.uk/guide/publishing-a-html-mini-site-to-moodle/)\n\n:::\n\n\n\n### Updating a Moodle-hosted site\n\nWhen you make changes to your Quarto project:\n\n1. Re-render the site locally.\n2. Create a new ZIP archive of the updated output.\n3. Replace the all existing files in Moodle.\n4. Ensure the correct `index.html` file remains set as the Main File.\n\n## Public Resources: GitHub Pages\n\nIf your Quarto content is intended for public access, GitHub Pages is an excellent option.\n\nAdvantages include:\n\n- Free hosting.\n- Integrated version control through Git.\n- Collaborative authoring workflows.\n- Automatic publishing when content is updated.\n- Suitable for open educational resources, documentation, and public-facing guides.\n\nGitHub Pages is particularly useful for resources that do not need Moodle authentication and are intended to be shared publicly.\n","srcMarkdownNoYaml":""},"formats":{"html":{"identifier":{"display-name":"HTML","target-format":"html","base-format":"html"},"execute":{"fig-width":7,"fig-height":5,"fig-format":"retina","fig-dpi":96,"df-print":"default","error":false,"eval":true,"cache":null,"freeze":false,"echo":true,"output":true,"warning":true,"include":true,"keep-md":false,"keep-ipynb":false,"ipynb":null,"enabled":null,"daemon":null,"daemon-restart":false,"debug":false,"ipynb-filters":[],"ipynb-shell-interactivity":null,"plotly-connected":true,"engine":"markdown"},"render":{"keep-tex":false,"keep-typ":false,"keep-source":false,"keep-hidden":false,"prefer-html":false,"output-divs":true,"output-ext":"html","fig-align":"default","fig-pos":null,"fig-env":null,"code-fold":false,"code-overflow":"wrap","code-link":false,"code-line-numbers":false,"code-tools":false,"tbl-colwidths":"auto","merge-includes":true,"inline-includes":false,"preserve-yaml":false,"latex-auto-mk":true,"latex-auto-install":true,"latex-clean":true,"latex-min-runs":1,"latex-max-runs":10,"latex-makeindex":"makeindex","latex-makeindex-opts":[],"latex-tlmgr-opts":[],"latex-input-paths":[],"latex-output-dir":null,"link-external-icon":false,"link-external-newwindow":false,"self-contained-math":false,"format-resources":[],"notebook-links":tru